WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:05.600
Larry Wilson, Screenwriter: There are architects
and there are gardeners.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:12.410
An architect will need that blueprint to build
what it is they are going to build.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.230
He said “I’m a gardener.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:18.500
I plant a seed and I tend it and nurse it
and watch it grow.”

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.009
Outlining A Screenplay - Architects and Gardeners

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:27.109
Danny Strong, Screenwriter/Producer/Actor:
My outlines are about 25 pages to 30 pages

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:28.109
long.

00:00:28.109 --> 00:00:34.110
Barrington: Even if I go in thinking I know
what it is, I’m still going to end up figuring

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.110
it out.

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:37.460
Gary Goldstein, Los Angeles Times movie reviewer/playwright/screenwriter:
Three sequences in the first act, 6 in the

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.960
second act and 3 in the third act of equal
weight.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.010
Blayne Weaver, Actor, Filmmaker, Screenwriter:
When you are trying to problem-solve instead

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:47.329
of trying to tell the most compelling story
that’s what I hate about outlining.

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:50.600
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter: It’s probably
50, 60, 70 percent of the work that you do

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:56.949
because it makes the load a lot easier and
you can write a faster screenplay if you have

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:57.949
an outline.

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:00.109
Tamika Lamison, Screenwriter: My body and
my brain just go “Just write it.

00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:01.109
It’s right there.

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:02.129
What is this outline stuff?”

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:07.200
Adam Skelter, Author/Screenwriter/Story Artist:
I’ll usually have a complete full outline

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.190
and then jump into the story with a really
specific idea of the objective in every single

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:12.190
scene.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:13.690
Markus Redmond, Filmmaker/Screenwriter/Actor:
You should make an outline, make your character

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:18.270
breakdowns and all these things that you do
and make your cards, plan out your story,

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.650
your first, second, and third…I don’t
do any of that.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:23.650
Architects

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.030
Danny Strong: I will say one thing that I
think could be very interesting to people

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:37.020
that are writers is what I do is I outline
a script very meticulously and my outlines

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.490
are about 25 pages to 30 pages long.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:46.640
And then when I go to write the actual first
draft, I will not reread one word of what

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:50.220
I’ve written until I’ve written the whole
script.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:55.460
So for my first pass on a screenplay, I start
from the beginning and then I just go…and

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:01.010
I go all the way through until I’m done
without having read one word of it and it

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.860
takes about three weeks.

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.404
So in three weeks, I’ve written a whole
draft and it’s a whole draft and it’s

00:02:05.404 --> 00:02:08.450
a lot of fun because there is no judgment
whatsoever.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:10.760
I’m just writing straight through.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:17.260
And then I go through and very meticulously
work through the scenes and that takes two

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:22.500
to three weeks and then in six weeks I have
a draft but it’s really two drafts because

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.379
I’ve done one straight through and then
one (this sort of meticulous work-through).

00:02:27.379 --> 00:02:32.170
And I will say that the second phase of it
(the meticulous work-through phase) is the

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:37.390
least enjoyable part of the process because
it’s the most critical where for a couple

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:42.930
weeks I just I have to work through these
scenes but I just do it.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.819
And then after that I have a draft to work
off of and then there are many drafts after

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:47.819
that.

00:02:47.819 --> 00:02:49.299
But I have this nice foundation.

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:54.379
Adam Skelter: I’m pretty thorough with outlining.

00:02:54.379 --> 00:03:01.780
I’ll spend quite a bit of time outlining
before I even write the full…like I’ll

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:05.290
usually have a complete full outline and then
jump into the story.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:11.069
So I’ll have a really specific idea of the
objective of every single scene and ultimately

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:12.830
it saves me time.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:17.749
When I first started writing I was anti-outline.

00:03:17.749 --> 00:03:22.269
I kind of had the attitude, you know the young
attitude I’m just going to write and my

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:26.340
gut’s going to tell me what’s right and
what’s meaningful and stuff like that and

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.200
that works for a lot of writers.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.109
But I noticed I hit a lot of dead ends and
I became emotionally attached to those dead

00:03:33.109 --> 00:03:38.319
ends and then it became very hard to rewrite
and then it drove my stories off the cliff.

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:43.590
And so little by little, honestly that Ron
Mita 24-plot-point-thing was huge because

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.930
it was so simple.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:51.040
You could literally just sit there and plot
out a story in an hour and you could have

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:52.890
a really good idea about where it was going.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:59.190
And then from there…for me that’s where
you go from like discovery your story to actually

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:03.262
becoming a craftsman (or craftsperson) where
you are working on the story and start to

00:04:03.262 --> 00:04:06.739
take control of it and you take a step back.

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:12.620
It’s almost like a render machine, you’re
rendering your story before you ever sit down

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:13.620
and write it.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.940
So then the script really just becomes kind
of like secondary artifact of all the work

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.640
you’ve put into it.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.660
So yeah, I spend a lot of time outlining.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:30.260
And it’s…a lot of people thinks that it
takes away from the creative process.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:33.450
Usually that’s kind of the writer saying
“Well, I want to be in the audience, too.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.570
And I want to experience and be surprised
by it.”

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:40.170
But outlining doesn’t take away from that
because when you sit down you’ve outlined,

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.780
you’ve got your outline right here.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:45.470
And then when you’re writing, you put yourself
in the moment, you’re in the skin of the

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:47.350
writer or the character.

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:53.841
And you are experiencing it and then if your
gut tells you try something different, try

00:04:53.841 --> 00:04:56.060
something different, see where it goes.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:02.730
So you are still…you know…gardener versus
architect, passer versus plotter kind of thing.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:11.410
I call it The Assassins versus The Berserkers.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:17.160
Basically the planner versus the person who
just sees what happens next.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:24.500
And I think it’s important to understand
the consequences of the choices you’re making.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:28.940
But the whole process of writing is an experiment,
art is an experiment.

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:34.190
The only thing that rules it is do we make
it emotionally resonate for somebody else.

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.660
And as artists we get to decide how much that
is.

00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:39.510
How we build that relationship.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:40.510
If.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:45.150
We want to do it for a living than we become
responsible to those people who are investing

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:50.780
in us and that’s when you want to be able
to have control of your craft.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.720
That’s when you want to be able to climb
the mountain, know how you’re going to get

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.940
up there, meet a deadline.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.390
There aren’t a lot of producers that are
going to be like “Alright, we needed this

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.520
three months ago.”

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.480
And you are still just kind of like wandering
in the weeds.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.850
But a lot of the process of writing is just
experimenting into the weeds, going into the

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:10.690
weeds and then stepping back.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:16.280
And the trick is just not panicking when you’re
in the weeds and saying “Okay, this is part

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.280
of the process.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.680
Let’s step back and look at the outline
and see if it’s going to work.”

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.220
So for me it feels like a map, it feels like
you’re zooming in.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:30.520
And you can do the street view where you are
in the character and you’re driving along

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.110
and you’re like “Yeah, I feel this, I’m
going through this street.”

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.410
And that’s when you’re writing.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:39.540
But then you pull back, you zoom out and you
see the overview of where you’re going and

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:40.780
that’s the outline.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:46.140
Mark Sanderson: Well for me on these assignments
that I do they are usually 10 or 12 pages.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:50.760
It’s the entire story, treatment and it’s
not just line by line.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.790
I mean there are some outlines that it’s
just a line literally.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:07:01.700
But for television they’re 8 acts so everything
has to be broken down but I write it as a

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:06.520
story so if you were to sit down and read
it, you’d be reading the story of the movie

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:11.950
which I think is essential to create, in fact
I’ve had friends that do this “Oh, I’m

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:15.400
just going to sit down and I have the vague
idea and I’m just going to write.”

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:20.440
And I’m like “You’ll be lost in the
barren wasteland of Act 2.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.540
With no water, you’ll be in the desert lost
and you’ll wonder how you’re going to

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:27.260
trudge through the 75-pages or whatever.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:36.460
I’m a huge advocate of starting outlines
before starting writing and I know it’s

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:43.500
probably 50, 60, 70 percent of the work because
it makes the load a lot easier and you can

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.170
write a faster screenplay if you have an outline.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:52.870
Now that doesn’t say there is no room for
changes or improvising but if you don’t

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:58.060
have a solid roadmap going in…it’s almost
like a pre-draft of a first draft.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:03.330
And I’m not an advocate of what people say
the vomit draft or just spill it out.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:07.990
But I don’t have the luxury of spilling
it out on my assignment jobs, I don’t.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:15.730
I really have to turn in let’s say on a
ten scale turn in probably an eight…an eight

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:22.360
out of ten…because I’m now holding up
development and I’ve also done rewrites

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:30.580
on other screenwriter’s work/projects that
means that the script that they had went through

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.810
multiple drafts and still is not there.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:40.380
And they have a buyer, they have network who
is waiting on the script and so I’ve also

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:45.399
been hired to do rewrite job where I can come
in but it’s like a page one rewrite where

00:08:45.399 --> 00:08:49.160
see the script that you have you can’t use
any of it, the names yes, the concept yes

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:56.100
but you basically…but that’s something
that I’ve learned how to do which is good

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:03.490
because there is a lot of rewrite work out
there and some writers look down on it.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:09.399
An example is that I took a rewrite job and
that ended up being three more jobs for the

00:09:09.399 --> 00:09:15.350
same company because that opened the door
for them and they were thankful for that.

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:24.279
But you never know what opportunity you’re
going to either turn down or accept.

00:09:24.279 --> 00:09:29.470
But back to your point about the outline,
I think it’s extremely important to do an

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:33.839
outline before your screenplay because it’s
easier to work out the problems there than

00:09:33.839 --> 00:09:38.220
it is writing a complete first draft and just
having so many problems.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:44.120
And also it trains you like I said, like you’re
an olympic athlete, it trains you for the

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.800
time that you do have to do outlines because
I’m not allowed to write these assignments

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:51.459
without an outline that I have to create.

00:09:51.459 --> 00:09:52.810
So they just wont let me go to pages.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:56.199
They have other people involved who have to
okay it.

00:09:56.199 --> 00:09:57.949
So the outline is extremely important.

00:09:57.949 --> 00:10:01.871
Gary Goldstein: Generally what I’ll do is
start with the premise line and I’ll write

00:10:01.871 --> 00:10:08.819
out a premise line basically the foundation
of the script, a couple of sentences from

00:10:08.819 --> 00:10:16.740
start-to-finish and then I’ll expand that
into maybe two or three pages, I’ll sort

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.129
of write it in sections.

00:10:18.129 --> 00:10:25.029
So I’ll divide it into (basically if it’s
a movie) three movie acts but more in quarters

00:10:25.029 --> 00:10:28.559
so it’s the equivalent of quarter, quarter,
quarter, quarter which takes me through the

00:10:28.559 --> 00:10:36.420
four quarters or the three acts of the movie
because one of the things that happens when

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:48.600
you start writing an outline in prose form
for me if I don’t stop writing at a certain

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:53.410
point you end up with let’s say you are
writing an outline and it’s only four pages.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:59.079
The first two pages may only be the first
quarter of the script because you end up writing

00:10:59.079 --> 00:11:10.879
so much and then ultimately you end with disproportionate
acts so basically I’m just trying to write

00:11:10.879 --> 00:11:12.959
it as tightly as I can.

00:11:12.959 --> 00:11:18.819
I’ll write it long and then I’ll edit
it but in the end I’ll up with four relatively

00:11:18.819 --> 00:11:22.810
close descriptions of each quarter.

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:28.050
So let’s say that ends up being three or
four pages whatever, then if it’s an assignment

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:31.379
or whatever then I’ll give it to the executive
or whatever, I’ll get notes on it, I’ll

00:11:31.379 --> 00:11:34.930
rework it and if it’s not for me, I’ll
do the same thing for myself.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:41.450
Then what I’ll do is I divide…and this
is I have to credit Writer’s Bootcamp for

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:45.590
this because this is the way they taught me
how to do it and how I always do it now and

00:11:45.590 --> 00:11:49.329
how I taught other people and I think it’s
pretty much one of those things that sort

00:11:49.329 --> 00:11:54.459
of, I don’t think it’s an uncommon way
to do it but it kind of boils it down to a

00:11:54.459 --> 00:12:00.499
really effective way of doing it is I divide
it up into 12 sequences.

00:12:00.499 --> 00:12:05.509
Three sequences in the first act, six in the
second act and three in the third act of equal

00:12:05.509 --> 00:12:11.620
weight so basically if you are writing a 120-page
script and it’s 10 pages of sequence.

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:17.670
If it’s more like a 100-page script it’s
8-9 pages of sequence and I beat out the list

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:21.199
of scenes.

00:12:21.199 --> 00:12:25.619
I create a sequence sentence, so this is sequence
one, two and three.

00:12:25.619 --> 00:12:30.019
So out of that sequence sentence that I write
out pulled from the outline (the longer outline)

00:12:30.019 --> 00:12:36.899
I then write a list of scenes that will comprise
that sequence.

00:12:36.899 --> 00:12:41.480
So I’ll put all the scenes in the row and
then I’ll figure out how many pages that

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:47.930
scene will approximately so that I can add
up and say at the end those scenes will equal

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:51.959
about 8 and a half pages or something and
it’s an estimate (sometimes I am very off)

00:12:51.959 --> 00:12:53.059
but it’s an estimate.

00:12:53.059 --> 00:12:58.649
And this is just my kind of organized way
of doing it and how I don’t get lost in

00:12:58.649 --> 00:13:01.310
the process and how I really kind of stay
on track.

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:02.310
This is just mine.

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:07.779
Everyone has their way of doing it and that’s
how I do it so you basically end up with 12

00:13:07.779 --> 00:13:16.110
series of scene lists so ultimately when you
add it all up it should equal whatever your

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:20.740
desired page count is, 100, 105, 120 pages
(some estimate).

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:27.569
Then I take that and put it on my Final Draft
(that list) and I create a slug line over

00:13:27.569 --> 00:13:30.980
each one INTERIOR: Living Room, Night.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.690
EXTERIOR: Baseball field day.

00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:39.839
So I have the line and then what that scene
is about and then I have the slug line.

00:13:39.839 --> 00:13:46.380
So basically I have a whole written script
basically except without dialogue essentially.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:49.069
And then you go in and you craft it.

00:13:49.069 --> 00:13:52.579
To me it’s a very step-by-step process like
an accordion.

00:13:52.579 --> 00:13:56.569
You are constantly expanding the accordion
until you have the whole script written.

00:13:56.569 --> 00:14:02.559
So that works for me but everybody does it
differently.

00:14:02.559 --> 00:14:07.720
Mark Sanderson: There is a step outline, there
is a one sheet, with just a one sheet of paper

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:10.589
of which is the concept, sort of a short synopsis.

00:14:10.589 --> 00:14:20.790
There is the longline which is even smaller
(two or three sentences) because you have

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:23.779
to quick pitch “Hhmmm…I’m not interested.”

00:14:23.779 --> 00:14:25.790
Then you have the pitch and they go “Hhmmm?

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:27.399
I’m willing to read something.

00:14:27.399 --> 00:14:29.769
Not the script but I’ll read the synopsis.”

00:14:29.769 --> 00:14:37.829
Then there are different steps of how interested
they are to read what you want, the final

00:14:37.829 --> 00:14:41.589
product, the final script is for them to read.

00:14:41.589 --> 00:14:45.170
But the treatment, there are many different.

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:50.879
Again, like a step outline, I’ve done outlines
that were 40 pages.

00:14:50.879 --> 00:14:54.569
I mean completely, triple it and you have
the script.

00:14:54.569 --> 00:15:00.129
But everything had to be worked out because
it was very technical the way the film was

00:15:00.129 --> 00:15:06.060
going to be shot was like from an iPhone and
from different things so it really had to

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:12.160
be spelled out because on set you really just
can’t leave it up to chance.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:16.029
But after that point I could have written
the script in weeks because it was all figured

00:15:16.029 --> 00:15:18.660
out, I had figured it out already.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:23.180
You want that outline for those dark periods
where ups are stuck.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:29.350
You don’t want to be stuck trying to figure
out plot points when you should be filling

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:30.350
pages.

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.980
That’s the worst part, that’s the worst
place to be.

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:34.149
“Gardeners”

00:15:34.149 --> 00:15:41.190
Larry Wilson: I will if contractually obliged
to doing a beat sheet or an outline, I’ll

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.879
do it and sometimes you are.

00:15:43.879 --> 00:15:48.639
And what usually happens is you spend all
of this time on this beat sheet and outline

00:15:48.639 --> 00:15:53.100
and it’s the most bastardized thing in the
world because it’s not a script, it’s

00:15:53.100 --> 00:16:00.060
not a story, it’s kind of this thing that
exists half way between a script and a story.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:03.490
But I will do it if contractually obliged
to, I’ll do an outline, I’ll do a very

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:06.989
detailed outline and I’ll do it to the best
of my abilities.

00:16:06.989 --> 00:16:09.899
I won’t slum but I’ll do it and I’ll
hand it in.

00:16:09.899 --> 00:16:13.179
And the producers will go “We love this
outline!

00:16:13.179 --> 00:16:14.209
This is a great outline.

00:16:14.209 --> 00:16:15.790
Now go write the script.”

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:19.610
And you’ll go write the script and you’ll
have your outline and you’ll say “Okay,

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:22.720
here is what the outline is telling me”
and you’ll start writing it and it will

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:29.300
immediately start changing and better ideas
will emerge and the next thing you know the

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:33.111
outline is completely out the window and you’ve
written a script and you’ll turn it in and

00:16:33.111 --> 00:16:35.749
the producers will go “We love this!

00:16:35.749 --> 00:16:37.170
This is just like the outline.”

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:38.869
And everyone has forgotten the outline

00:16:38.869 --> 00:16:47.800
And I know…my beat sheet is obviously you
kind of know the beginning.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:53.939
You know feel these characters and you are
putting them in this situation where they

00:16:53.939 --> 00:16:58.769
are gong to have to fight for their lives
to get out of.

00:16:58.769 --> 00:17:04.141
I kind of know what the end is going to be
(kind of, sort of) and then it’s the great

00:17:04.141 --> 00:17:06.050
unknown.

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:11.220
And to do that in a beat sheet to me is just
taking all of the fun out of it.

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:17.630
So don’t listen to me, listen to all of
the other people out there who say outline

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:18.939
beat sheets.

00:17:18.939 --> 00:17:20.750
Please if that is what you need to do.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:26.699
Are there great writers who do that, of course
they do and sometimes when you’re collaborating

00:17:26.699 --> 00:17:34.000
it becomes more necessary because you are
sending stuff back and forth but for me, they

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:35.640
don’t work for me.

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:42.220
And any time I do them, everything starts
changing anyway so much that they quickly

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:43.220
become irrelevant.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:49.279
Markus Redmond, Writer/Director/Actor: Gosh
this is going to sound terrible…there are

00:17:49.279 --> 00:17:51.830
things that you are supposed to do as a writer
that you should do.

00:17:51.830 --> 00:17:55.711
You should make an outline, you should make
your character breakdowns, there are all of

00:17:55.711 --> 00:17:57.039
these things that you do.

00:17:57.039 --> 00:18:00.919
You make your cards and you plot out your
story (your first and second and third).

00:18:00.919 --> 00:18:02.580
I don’t do any of that.

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:03.580
None of it!

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:04.670
Film Courage: That is the perfect screenwriter.

00:18:04.670 --> 00:18:07.170
That’s the one that has the perfect sock
drawer.

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:11.440
Markus: Yes, that’s the guy who lays it
all correctly out.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:12.440
I don’t do any of that.

00:18:12.440 --> 00:18:18.740
I like to sit and think about the idea and
then I like to think about the character.

00:18:18.740 --> 00:18:25.320
Who that woman or man is and what they’re
like, what their world looks like, and then

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:30.299
I just start to think about what the movie
might look like in my head.

00:18:30.299 --> 00:18:35.180
And once I start seeing the movie itself in
my head, then I start writing it.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:39.809
I don’t know how it’s going to end necessarily…unless
it’s a thriller!

00:18:39.809 --> 00:18:43.789
If it’s a thriller, I need to know how it’s
going to end so I can know where I’ve got

00:18:43.789 --> 00:18:46.480
to go if that makes sense?

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:49.960
Because you always want to have that ending
so people go “Whoah!”

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:53.060
So you think of the ending first like what
would be a cool “Whoah!”

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:55.840
Oooh, okay how do I get there?

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:58.070
So I go backward if it’s a thriller.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:03.960
If it’s not a thriller, if it’s just a
story about people and life, love whatever

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:09.023
it is then I don’t really think that much
about what is going to happen in the story.

00:19:09.023 --> 00:19:12.950
I think about the people and where they are
and where I want them to be, what the world

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:14.159
is like, what they enjoy.

00:19:14.159 --> 00:19:23.730
There is usually a lot of wine involved because
I enjoy some wine and what happens is I end

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:35.970
up writing something that is much more organic
and much more…here I am a writer and I can’t

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:40.210
even find a word…uh…what is the word?

00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:46.630
Organic and…what is the word when just…spontaneous!

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:54.140
Organic and spontaneous, I end up writing
something that is organic and spontaneous

00:19:54.140 --> 00:19:56.679
because I haven’t sort of telegraphed it.

00:19:56.679 --> 00:20:00.760
Kind of like when you’re acting you always
hear “Don’t Play Result.”

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:05.260
Because you’ve read the entire script so
your character is on a journey so you know

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:07.669
what that journey is but your character doesn’t
know.

00:20:07.669 --> 00:20:11.140
So you don’t want to play like you know
what is coming because you don’t want to

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:12.989
telegraph it to the audience.

00:20:12.989 --> 00:20:20.200
I find the same thing works for me as a writer
by not knowing everything when I’m writing,

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:25.190
I’m discovering it as I’m writing and
it makes the writing process exciting for

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:30.460
me because I’m sitting there at the keyboard
like “This is great!”

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:33.110
Your like “What is going to happen next?”

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:34.190
And you’re like “I don’t know!

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:35.190
I can’t wait to find out either.”

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:36.190
You know what I mean?

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:39.210
It’s a spontaneous thing.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:44.639
And then hopefully the training and the laws
of inevitability that I try to keep focused

00:20:44.639 --> 00:20:52.139
in my head kick in as I’m writing and when
it’s done it’s a really great story.

00:20:52.139 --> 00:21:00.539
But yes, I like to look at it as a living,
breathing story that is unfolding as if I

00:21:00.539 --> 00:21:07.679
am watching it and that drives me toward the
end because I feel like if I do all of the

00:21:07.679 --> 00:21:12.360
outline stuff (for me) if I do all of the
outline stuff, I’ve already done it already

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:16.570
so now I’m like “Aaargh, now I’ve got
to write it all?

00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:18.389
I already figured it out.”

00:21:18.389 --> 00:21:20.360
So I just jump in and start writing.

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:25.720
Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn, Screenwriter/Author:
It’s like a discovery when you have preconceptions

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:30.230
of what your story is but then it develops
into something very different.

00:21:30.230 --> 00:21:35.760
I think there is something very exciting about
that and something very freeing about it if

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:42.110
you allow yourself to open yourself to that
you can go in the direction because I think

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:47.980
to a certain extent some of the things that
we’ve done (at least in this screenplay)

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:53.899
have moved further from what I thought my
original idea was but it’s so much better.

00:21:53.899 --> 00:21:58.289
Blayne Weaver, Writer/Director/Actor: I’d
rather not but usually that is required for

00:21:58.289 --> 00:21:59.289
the job.

00:21:59.289 --> 00:22:05.549
Like if I’m getting paid for it, they will
want to know…these days to get hired to

00:22:05.549 --> 00:22:14.460
write a script you pretty much have to 35-page
outline for a 90-page script, turn it in before

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:17.470
you get hired to write the script.

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:22.200
So when I’m writing for other people I do
whatever it takes to make them comfortable

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:26.230
with it which normally involves an online.

00:22:26.230 --> 00:22:31.970
Me…I’m not a musician but for me I feel
like it’s just jazz.

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:40.630
I feel when it’s time for a change, I can
feel when we need to know more about a character.

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:46.750
My way of working it’s very artificial to
say “at this point on page 12 you get to

00:22:46.750 --> 00:22:49.289
learn that Charlie has a secret.”

00:22:49.289 --> 00:22:50.289
You know what I mean?

00:22:50.289 --> 00:22:51.720
Because I don’t know what secret is?

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:57.590
Do I make it up or do I wait to get to know
who Charlie is and write that in?

00:22:57.590 --> 00:23:04.399
I feel like the music of it…I know on page
12 maybe there is something he is not sharing.

00:23:04.399 --> 00:23:06.701
But maybe it is this?

00:23:06.701 --> 00:23:08.419
It’s just way more organic and I feel like
it flows.

00:23:08.419 --> 00:23:13.269
A lot of times with outlines I’ll find when
I’m writing the actual script I won’t

00:23:13.269 --> 00:23:17.409
feel it going the way the outline was, you
know what I mean?

00:23:17.409 --> 00:23:21.419
So it’s either like I do something false.

00:23:21.419 --> 00:23:26.669
Writing for me, a lot of times (especially
the rewrite process for other people’s work)

00:23:26.669 --> 00:23:29.549
is taking a round peg and putting it into
a square hole.

00:23:29.549 --> 00:23:36.460
It’s like here is a movie (this is an actual
true story), I had written a film that was

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:45.460
a beach sport kind of romantic comedy that
was set in Thailand.

00:23:45.460 --> 00:23:47.220
Great, love it!

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:51.429
Only now it’s in Bulgaria and it’s Christmas.

00:23:51.429 --> 00:23:58.309
So my rewrite was to completely and utterly
change the entire scenery and what is happening

00:23:58.309 --> 00:24:03.320
(round peg, square hole) and it’s like a
Rubik’s cube kind of problem.

00:24:03.320 --> 00:24:05.159
It doesn’t necessarily lead to good writing.

00:24:05.159 --> 00:24:11.700
When you are trying to problem solve instead
of telling the most compelling story, that

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:12.700
is what I hate about outlining.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:19.540
That’s why I don’t like being pushed into
a corner by some random decision I made three

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:22.880
months ago when I was just trying to make
people feel comfortable about what the movie

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:24.799
was going to be, does that make sense?

00:24:24.799 --> 00:24:28.279
I can ramble about it for the next two hours
because it’s something that I struggle with

00:24:28.279 --> 00:24:29.279
a lot.

00:24:29.279 --> 00:24:32.740
Tamika Lamison, Screenwriter: I’m not someone
who does, even thought my first successful

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:37.370
screenplay was almost like doing an outline,
it was like a stream of consciousness treatment

00:24:37.370 --> 00:24:42.890
that I wrote and then I realized after writing
like 20 or 30 pages “You should just write

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:43.890
the script.

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:44.890
It’s right here.

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:45.890
You should just write the script.”

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:50.480
But that was because I had been marinating
on it so long I could just do that.

00:24:50.480 --> 00:24:56.090
But when it comes to outlines which is a process
that a lot of script writers do and I’m

00:24:56.090 --> 00:25:01.980
still trying to figure out how to do that,
I really admire people who know how to outline

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:03.130
and write their scripts from there.

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:08.909
Things are just a little more organic for
me but I can write a beginning, middle and

00:25:08.909 --> 00:25:13.640
end but it usually is two or three pages of
stuff and a character breakdown.

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:20.880
I often feel like if I had the…I don’t
even know what it is?

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:26.340
Whatever it is that a screenwriter has that
makes them disciplined and do that outline,

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:29.090
I think that my job might be a little bit
easier.

00:25:29.090 --> 00:25:32.480
I’m just saying that because it seems like
that works really well.

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.769
It’s like the studio way of doing things
too.

00:25:34.769 --> 00:25:37.889
Like in TV you have to give an outline and
then you write the script.

00:25:37.889 --> 00:25:40.380
Oh, I will tell you a funny story about that.

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:47.419
So I was in the Walt Disney Fellowship and
I was in the screenwriting version of it.

00:25:47.419 --> 00:25:51.429
They don’t have the screenwriting arm anymore,
they just have the TV arm believe it or not.

00:25:51.429 --> 00:25:58.019
I’ve been trying to get the other one back
but I wrote a television script for them and

00:25:58.019 --> 00:26:03.710
at the time it was THE PRACTICE and MY WIFE
AND KIDS were the TV shows that I wanted to

00:26:03.710 --> 00:26:04.909
write for.

00:26:04.909 --> 00:26:10.200
And they were like “Okay so you have to
pitch us your story, do an outline, once we

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:12.960
like story and then write the script.”

00:26:12.960 --> 00:26:15.399
So I pitched them the story they liked it,
right?

00:26:15.399 --> 00:26:18.730
And I was just like “What’s this outline
thing?

00:26:18.730 --> 00:26:20.669
I don’t know what this is?”

00:26:20.669 --> 00:26:26.840
I literally wrote the script, then I wrote
the outline from the script, and then I gave

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:28.929
them the outline and they were like “This
is great.”

00:26:28.929 --> 00:26:36.800
They made a couple of tweaks in the outline,
so I implemented the tweaks into the script.

00:26:36.800 --> 00:26:38.519
The script was already done.

00:26:38.519 --> 00:26:40.650
Film Courage: So it was just like a formality?

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:44.231
Tamika: It was a formality because that was
the way they had to do it.

00:26:44.231 --> 00:26:48.460
But it didn’t make organic sense to my process.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:50.789
Barrington Smith-Seetachit, Screenwriter:
Really if I’m honest, I’m always figuring

00:26:50.789 --> 00:26:58.320
it out as I write even if I go in thinking
that I know what it is, I’m still going

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:11.070
to end up figuring it out as I go…Yeah…I
have inside-out writing and outside-in writing

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:18.710
and the outside-in will tend to be something
where there is kind of an external story and

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:24.159
so you’ve got the container a little bit
and then you’re figuring out all of the

00:27:24.159 --> 00:27:34.169
ways, furnishing and arranging in that container
and generating some new stuff.

00:27:34.169 --> 00:27:41.570
Occasionally I have the luxury of doing what
I call the inside-out where you have almost

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:42.570
nothing.

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:47.120
You have like…you woke up in the middle
of the night with like an image in your head

00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:53.929
or just kind of this fragmentary thing like
a grain of sand and then it’s like you’re

00:27:53.929 --> 00:27:55.250
making narrative stone soup.

00:27:55.250 --> 00:28:00.690
You are like “Here is the pebble,” and
do you know the story of STONE SOUP?

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:07.100
And then they go around, the meat and the
carrots and then it’s more like…it’s

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:13.920
like found found art pulling all of the things
from your life, your memories, what’s going

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:21.169
on and you’re kind of building it out from
this little thing and it becomes something

00:28:21.169 --> 00:28:26.309
where you really didn’t have much of an
idea of what it’s going to be at all and

00:28:26.309 --> 00:28:30.820
so that’s pretty fun.

00:28:30.820 --> 00:28:36.710
And that of course would be an example of
figuring it out as you go.

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:45.500
Larry Wilson, Screenwriter/Instructor: I’m
a storyteller and the stories emerge from

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:50.990
some place that is beat sheet proof, that’s
just me.

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:53.480
I mean, I don’t know.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:56.440
They just come from some where else.

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:05.539
And if I tell is all in a quote-un-quote beat
sheet it’s like why would I do that to myself.

00:29:05.539 --> 00:29:10.980
Why would I take all of the fun out of discovering
it and put it in this thing that is just kind

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:19.790
of bullet points, but that is me and that
is the idiosyncratic part of me.

00:29:19.790 --> 00:29:26.389
But I’m in good company (Stephen King) my
favorite writer.

00:29:26.389 --> 00:29:33.480
Ask him about beat sheets.

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:40.140
But help me…that writer that wrote GAME
OF THRONES and the books [off-camera: “George

00:29:40.140 --> 00:29:41.200
R.R. Martin”].

00:29:41.200 --> 00:29:42.260
Thank you!

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:45.300
He said it the best of anyone.

00:29:45.300 --> 00:29:52.419
He said there are…now you would think if
you had read the Game of Thrones books that

00:29:52.419 --> 00:29:56.409
those things are outlined completely.

00:29:56.409 --> 00:29:57.450
They are so intricate.

00:29:57.450 --> 00:30:04.460
They are so full of plot and character and
this need to overlap and this needs to…I

00:30:04.460 --> 00:30:08.820
mean they are extremely intricate stories.

00:30:08.820 --> 00:30:14.309
He doesn’t outline and what he says about
it (and I think it’s such a great quote

00:30:14.309 --> 00:30:17.149
and I’m paraphrasing a little bit).

00:30:17.149 --> 00:30:20.909
“There are architects and there are gardeners.

00:30:20.909 --> 00:30:27.720
An architect will need that blueprint to build
what it is they are going to build.”

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:29.539
He said “I’m a gardener.

00:30:29.539 --> 00:30:36.759
I plant a seed and I tend it and nurse it
and watch it grow.”

00:30:36.759 --> 00:30:42.039
There are architects that will do that beat
sheet and will do it brilliantly, so I am

00:30:42.039 --> 00:30:46.620
not knocking that and there are gardeners
and I guess I’m a gardener.

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:52.309
I just kind of let the idea sit there and
I tend it and I take good care of it and make

00:30:52.309 --> 00:30:54.950
sure it gets all of the water and sun and
I watch it grow.

00:30:54.950 --> 00:31:00.340
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: It does
allow you to write a faster first draft if

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:05.769
you have a solid outline because for me I
have to see the film in my head before I can

00:31:05.769 --> 00:31:10.929
write it and I know I have a lot of problems
ahead of me if I can’t see it.

00:31:10.929 --> 00:31:16.950
I mean I don’t lock in, like I almost see
the movie, if you watch a movie you remember

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:22.809
it, I have to see it that way and I know things
are working when…and you have to live…and

00:31:22.809 --> 00:31:27.720
the outline is good for living with your characters,
you get to live with them.

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:33.190
You get to see how the movie is working or
doesn’t work before you sit down and write

00:31:33.190 --> 00:31:35.159
that screenplay which is building the house.

00:31:35.159 --> 00:31:38.309
It’s almost like the pre-blueprint (in my
opinion).

00:31:38.309 --> 00:31:41.980
That’s been my experience but I know a lot
of writers just want to write down a couple

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:48.720
of lines and wing it and there are so many
things that can go wrong and why not turn

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:52.090
in the most amazing first draft you can.

00:31:52.090 --> 00:31:54.320
I don’t see the problem with that.

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:59.779
And in fact like I say you have to (when you
are doing assignment work) you want fewer

00:31:59.779 --> 00:32:03.360
drafts, you don’t want eight drafts because
nothing is working.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:05.710
You want to turn that in where they go “Wow!”

00:32:05.710 --> 00:32:08.650
My assignment is that I did two drafts and
two polishes, we’re done.

00:32:08.650 --> 00:32:10.370
That’s what they like.

00:32:10.370 --> 00:32:12.900
They don’t like five “You’re not getting
it.”

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:17.610
That’s where they get fired and they hire
somebody else where they can facilitate the

00:32:17.610 --> 00:32:20.149
notes and get it moving.

00:32:20.149 --> 00:32:23.440
It’s creative but it’s also a business
at the same time unfortunately.

00:32:23.440 --> 00:32:30.409
Tamika Lamison, Screenwriter: When I have
to I will but it’s very uncomfortable.

00:32:30.409 --> 00:32:33.480
I fell like my organic process is just different.

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:39.070
I understand why it works for people and I
understand why they do it, but my body and

00:32:39.070 --> 00:32:42.769
brain just goes “Just write it!

00:32:42.769 --> 00:32:43.960
It’s right here.

00:32:43.960 --> 00:32:44.980
What is this outline stuff?

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:45.980
You are wasting time.

00:32:45.980 --> 00:32:47.299
Just write the script.”

00:32:47.299 --> 00:32:50.029
Gary Goldstein, Screenwriter/Journalist: I
look at the Coen Brothers movies (some of

00:32:50.029 --> 00:32:56.350
the greatest movies of our time), amazing
stories, incredible character, incredibly

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:57.350
creative.

00:32:57.350 --> 00:33:01.460
Those movies are not (I don’t know how they
outline, if they outline) but they don’t

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:08.570
feel as specifically structured as most movies
you see (some are, some aren’t) they are

00:33:08.570 --> 00:33:09.570
really brilliant.

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:16.690
They are able to infuse their stories in their
own world and their own structure for example.

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:18.179
If you can do it, it’s great.

00:33:18.179 --> 00:33:24.049
I think I’ve just seen too many writers
who get lost in the process because they haven’t

00:33:24.049 --> 00:33:27.570
sort of laid it out ahead of time.

00:33:27.570 --> 00:33:32.899
But again it works for me and I never look
at it like “Oh it’s putting me in a box

00:33:32.899 --> 00:33:34.679
or using a formula.”

00:33:34.679 --> 00:33:39.769
Not at all, it’s just how I write and especially
when you are on a deadline.

00:33:39.769 --> 00:33:45.679
You have a limited amount of time to write
something, it really helps me move more quickly

00:33:45.679 --> 00:33:53.009
and gives me a system of sort of checks and
balances on my material and it helps me not

00:33:53.009 --> 00:33:59.910
overwrite as well and ultimately not lose
too much time on any material that I’m not

00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:00.910
ultimately going to put in the script.

00:34:00.910 --> 00:34:05.309
Adam Skelter, Writer/Author/Instructor: I
don’t believe in any right or wrong.

00:34:05.309 --> 00:34:09.290
There is this one writer Bob Sines, he is
amazing.

00:34:09.290 --> 00:34:13.909
He says he doesn’t outline, he is successful
and writes great stuff.

00:34:13.909 --> 00:34:17.790
He wrote Extracurricular and it’s fantastic.

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:20.300
I read the script, it’s so good!

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:26.169
And he doesn’t outline but he knows how
to write.

00:34:26.169 --> 00:34:30.450
He gets where he needs to go and he knows
how to tell a story.

00:34:30.450 --> 00:34:33.889
So it’s like what works for you is fine.

00:34:33.889 --> 00:34:39.829
The important thing is to be able to sell
it to the producers, to engage the people

00:34:39.829 --> 00:34:41.399
who need to be engaged.

00:34:41.399 --> 00:34:43.379
You are writing for readers largely.

00:34:43.379 --> 00:34:48.130
So you want to learn the language of what
you expect, but within that, it’s whatever

00:34:48.130 --> 00:34:49.130
gets you there.

00:34:49.130 --> 00:34:55.059
I don’t think you have any obligation, but
generally speaking the average person would

00:34:55.059 --> 00:34:59.829
benefit from taking the time to plan out where
there story is going to go and have a good

00:34:59.829 --> 00:35:04.201
idea of how it’s going to end and then work
toward that.

00:35:04.201 --> 00:35:06.270
A lot of people they will stare at the blank
page.

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:09.901
I’ve never understood staring at the blank
page because by the time I’m ready to write

00:35:09.901 --> 00:35:13.130
a scene, I’ve already had like pages of
writing.

00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:20.740
It’s like a slingshot, you are slowing preparing
getting all of the scenes, getting the character,

00:35:20.740 --> 00:35:24.240
getting the plot, the conflict, you know what
every character wants.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:31.290
So when you sit down and you’re ready to
write, you let go and you’re just flying.

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:34.130
But it really comes down to everybody discovering
their own process.

00:35:34.130 --> 00:35:38.920
I’m not a purest and lots of people don’t
outline and they are very good writers.

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:42.300
A lot of that is their intuition or their
experience that informs it.

